  "text_md": "# —\n\n;\n\n;\n\n;\n\n;\n\n!\n\n!\n\n![](<11_pg146_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\n![](<11_pg146_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\n## PES 618 PET\n\nto the helmet. \" A pesane and a paunsone,\"\n\nPERSPECTIVE. A reflecting-glass.\n\nMS. Morte Arthure, f. 89.\n\nPERSPICIL. An optic-glass. It occurs in\n\nPESATE. Is when a managed horse rises handsomely before and upon his haunches, and ax the same time bends hisfore-legs up tohisbody.\n\nAlbumazar, 1634, sig. B. iv.\n\nPERSTAND. To understand. Peek.\n\nPERSUADE. Persuasion.\n\nPESE. (1) Peace. Perceval 980, 981.\n\nPERSUADERS. Spurs. Also, pistols.\n\nPERSWAY. To mitigate. Ben Jonson, iv. 428. PERT. Beautifully delicate. It is the trans-\n\n(2) To sooth ; to appease. Tylle y be sewre of youre hartys ese. Nothing but hit may my grevys pese.\n\nlation of mbtilis in Gesta Rom. p. 142. For hete her clothes down sche dede\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. i. G. f. 132.\n\nPESEN. Peas. This is the common early form of the word, and occurs in Chaucer, Legende of Good Women, 648. Holloway gives the following couplet, as seen lately on a board in a pea-field in Berkshire\n\nAlmest to her gerdyl stede.\n\nThan lay sche uncovert\n\nSche was as whyt as lylye yn May, Or snow that sneweth yn wynterys day.\n\nHe seygh never non so pert.\n\nIllustrations of Fairy Mythology, p. 11. PERTE. (1) To part. Still in use.\n\nShut the gate after you, I'll tell you the reason. Because the pigs shouldn't get into the peason.\n\nThen Thomas a sory man was he.\n\nBen Jonson has made the same words rhyme in his 133d epigram.\n\nThe terys ran out of his een gray\n\nLufly lady, jet tell thou me\n\nAs for his sallets, better never was Then acute sorrell, and sweet three-leav'd grasse, And for a sawee he seldome is at charges. For every crab-tree doth aflfbord him vergis ;\n\nIf we shalle perte for ever and ay.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 125.\n\n(2) Of good appearance. Ther was no man in the kynges lande More perte then was he.\n\nHis banket sometimes is greene beanes and peason. Nuts, peares, plumbes, apples, as they are in season.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 244.\n\nTaijor's Workes, 1630, i. 97-\n\nPERTELICHE. Openly. (A.-N.)\n\nPESIBLE. Peaceable. {A.-N.)\n\nPESIBLETE. A calm. {A.-N.) PESK. A peach. Nominale MS.\n\nThan syr Priamous the prynce in presens of lordes\n\nPresez to his penowne, and per«i/ it hentes.\n\nMorte Arthure, MS. Lincoln, f. 84.\n\nPESON. An instrument in the form of a staff, with balls or crockets, used for weighing be-\n\nPERTELOTE. The name of a hen.\n\nPERTENERE. A partner.\n\nfore scales were employed.\n\nGod graunt us mekenesse in angurs here. And grace to lede owre lyfe here soo.\n\nPESS. A hassock. Suffolk.\n\nPESSCOD-SCALDING. A kind of merry-\n\nThat may aftur be pertenere\n\nmaking in summer evenings\n\n; the treat, green field peas boiled in the shells. Yorksh.\n\nOf hevene, whan we hens schall goo.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 14.\n\nPESSIPE. A kind of cup.\n\nPERTRYCHE. A partridge.\n\nPESTERED. Crowded. Peele, ii. 235.\n\nRyght as the pertryche is constreyned undir the claues and nayles of the hauke, is as halfe deed for drede. Caxton's Divers Fruyt/iil Ghostly Maters.\n\nPESTERMENT. Embarrassment. North.\n\nPESTLE. (1) A leg of an animal, generally of a pig. A pestle of pork is still in common use. \" Pestels of venison,\" Warner's Antiq. Culin. p. 98. *• Pestell of flesshe,^'amJon,\" Palsgrave.\n\nPERTURBE. To trouble. Palsgrave.\n\nPERTY. Pait. Lydgate.\n\nGod that sittis in Trinitd, Gyffe thaym grace wel to the. That lystyns me a whyle\n\nA pestle-pie is a large standing pie which contains a whole gammon, and sometimes a couple of fowls and a neat's tongue, a favorite dish at country fairs, and at Christmas feasts.\n\nAUe that lovys of melody,\n\nOflFhevon blisse God graunte tham perty,\n\nTheyrr soules shelde fro peryle.\n\n(2) A constable's staff. PESTLE-HEAD. A blockhead.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 47.\n\nPERUR. A kind of cup. PERUSE. To examine, or survey.\n\nPETE. Pity. See Cov. Myst. Long lay the kyng, there away wolde not hee\n\nMonsieur Soubiez having perused the fleet, returned to the king, and told him there waa nothing\n\nDayly he propherid batayle : the enmys durst not fyghte\n\nLacke of logynge and vitayle it was grett pete, Causid the gentill prynce to remeve, siche was Goddes\n\nand that the mariners and souldiers would not yeeld to goe the voyage till they were paid their\n\nready\n\n;\n\nmyjte\n\narrears. MS. HarU 383.\n\nLowe, how the good Lorde his owne gentill knyjte. Because he shulde remcmbir hym in wele and in woo. Thus in every thyng, Lorde, thy wille be doo\n\nPERVEY. To provide. (^.-A^.) PERVINKE. The herb periwinkle. {A.-S.)\n\nPERYE. A pear-tree. (A.-N.) But for hur lorde sche durste not done. That sate benethe and pleyed hym merye. Before the towre undur a perye.\n\nMS. Bibl. Reg. 17 D. xv.\n\nPETEOSE. Merciful ; compassionate.\n\nMany men spekes of lamentacioun, OfTmoders and of their gret desolatioun.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. ii. 38, f. 141.\n\nWhich that thay did indure\n\nPERYSSE. Pears. {A.-N.)\n\nWhen that their childer dy and passe, But of his peteose tender moder, alasre\n\nThen was the tre ful of ripe perysse, And began down to falle.\n\n•\n\nI am verray sure.\n\nMS. Cantab. Ff. v. 48, f. 114.\n\nThe wo and payn passis alle otherc.\n\nPESANE. A gorget of mail or plate attached\n\nMS. Bodl. e Mils, m<\n\n",
